<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Bardo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Kassar Said</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Bardo was signed on 12 May 1881 at the Ksar Saïd palace near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iii-as-sadiq">Muhammad as-Sadiq Bey</a>, under pressure from an invading French army. It placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">Beylik</a> of Tunis under French protection, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> control of its foreign affairs and defence while the bey kept his throne. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marsa-convention">La Marsa Convention</a> of 1883 extended French control to internal administration, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> lasted until 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo.jpg" alt="Treaty of Bardo" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gouvernement français + Le Bey de Tunis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trait%C3%A9_du_Bardo_de_1881_-_p3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
